<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">El-Kurru</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Kurru</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">El-Kurru is a royal cemetery near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napata">Napata</a> in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudan">Sudan</a>, burial place of the early kings of Kush. The tombs of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashta">Kashta</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piye">Piye</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shabaka">Shabaka</a>, Shebitku and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tantamani">Tantamani</a> lie there, with pyramids built in the Egyptian manner, and the kings' horses were buried nearby. It forms part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jebel-barkal">Gebel Barkal</a> and the Sites of the Napatan Region.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/el-kurru.jpg" alt="El-Kurru" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bertramz · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Al-Kurru,main_pyramid.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
